Subject: [PATCH] flash: let the host state whether the link is wireless

port_is_wireless() infers the link type from the port string. That is
right for a normal client, but wrong under a bridge that terminates the
real link itself and hands the client a loopback socket: USB, BT-classic
and BLE all present as "tcp:", so a USB-attached device is refused as
wireless and cannot be flashed at all.

PM3_LINK_WIRELESS lets the host say which it is. pm3-droid sets it from
the live transport, so a genuine wireless link still reports itself as
wireless and the bootloader guard still fires. Unset or unrecognised
falls through to the original inference.

--- a/client/src/flash.c
+++ b/client/src/flash.c
@@ -463,6 +463,23 @@
 }
 
 static bool port_is_wireless(const char *name) {
+    // PM3_LINK_WIRELESS: the host states the link type when it knows it.
+    //
+    // Inferring it from the port string breaks under a bridge that
+    // terminates the real link itself and hands this client a loopback
+    // socket -- USB, BT-classic and BLE then all present as "tcp:", so a
+    // USB-attached device is refused as wireless and cannot be flashed at
+    // all. The Android app sets this from the transport it actually opened.
+    //
+    // This makes the guard accurate, not absent: a wireless link sets 1 and
+    // is still refused by a bootloader that cannot stream over one. An unset
+    // or unrecognised value falls through to the inference below, so a normal
+    // build is unaffected.
+    const char *forced = getenv("PM3_LINK_WIRELESS");
+    if (forced != NULL && (forced[0] == '0' || forced[0] == '1') && forced[1] == '\0') {
+        return forced[0] == '1';
+    }
+
     if (name == NULL) {
         return false;
     }
